You are now in a READ-EVAL-PRINT loop, with the exception that certain 
atoms flag special actions. They are:
time	Print parse time for last sentence.
ctree	Prints case tree.
ftree	Prints annotated surface structure tree with labels.
tree	"         "        "        "        "  w\\o    "   .
ntree	"         "        "        "        "  with node names.
ltree   ntree + ftree
lisp    Flush this toplevel loop, restoring lisp's toplevel.
p	Call the Parser.
The parser prompts with >.
Type a sentence finishing with a final punctuation mark and then a <return>.
To repeat parsing of last sentence type <ESC> S <return>.
To trace parse type "(ptrace)".  To clear trace "(runfast)".
To trace case frame stuff "(ctrace t)". To clear "(ctrace nil)".
To complement "Movie" trace mode, type cntrl-G <return>.
	(In this state the atom PSWAIT determines the time between frames.
	 It can be set to either 0, in which case the parser runs
	 continuously, or NIL, in which case the parser will 
	 wait for a space to be typed between frames.)
	(typing "(noshow t)" surpresses display of most low level constituent
	construction. "(noshow nil)" will display everything.
	"(noshow packet1 packet2 .... packetn)" surpresses display 
	of any rules in these packets.)

To see parser state, type (pstate).

